  "question": {
    "text": "Two rigid boxes containing different ideal gases are placed on a table. Box <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A</mi></math> contains one mole of nitrogen at temperature <math><msub><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></math>, while box <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">B</mi></math> contains one mole of helium at temperature <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mn>7</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></math>. The boxes are then put into thermal contact with each other, and heat flows between them until the gases reach a common final temperature (Ignore the heat capacity of boxes). Then , the final temperature of the gases, <math><msub><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo></math> in terms of <math><msub><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></math> is",
    "image": null
  },
  "options": [
    {
      "id": "Wkeib",
      "text": "<math><msub><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>3</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>7</mn></mrow></mfrac><msub><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></math>",
      "image": null,
